## Primitive Data Types

A **primitive** data type holds a single value. Languages differ slightly in
which primitives they provide, but the common four you will be expected to
recognise are integer, floating-point, boolean, and character.

Choosing the right primitive matters because it affects memory, precision,
and what operations are valid. Storing a price as an integer number of pence
avoids the rounding errors that come with floating-point arithmetic, which
is why financial systems almost never store money as a float.

## Compound Data Types

A **compound** type groups multiple values under one name. The two patterns
you need to recognise are arrays and records.

An **array** holds many values of the same type, indexed by position. Think
of a shelf of identical boxes numbered from zero. You can access any box by
its number and you can iterate through all of them in order.

A **record** (sometimes called a struct, dictionary, or object depending on
the language) holds several related values of different types under named
fields. A customer record might have a `name` string, an `email` string, a
`joined_date`, and a boolean flag for whether they have verified their email.

## Why the Distinction Matters

Most real-world data uses both at once. A list of customers is an array of
records. A shopping basket is an array of records, where each record holds a
product reference and a quantity. A weather forecast is an array of records,
where each record holds a date, a temperature, and a description.

The examiner cue to reach for when you see "store many items, each with
several fields" is an array of records. If you name only an array, you miss
the point that each item has internal structure. If you name only a record,
you miss the point that there are many of them.
